  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Let $v=\u27e8-4,0,3\u27e9.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $v.$",
  "worked_solution": "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{|\\,v\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,v\\,| & =\\sqrt{{v}_{x}^{2}+{v}_{y}^{2}+{v}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+0+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $v$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{|\\,v\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \u27e8-4,0,3\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-\\frac{4}{5},0,\\frac{3}{5}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$",
